WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:08.600
We turned the ground into a sensor.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.900
Viewed from above, the paws of a walking rat are largely hidden beneath its body.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.900
The tactile floor records each contact: its location, its duration and the load it bears.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:26.800
Labels indicate automatic paw assignments (FL, FR, HL, HR).

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.000
Four 64 × 64 sensor modules form a 512 × 128 mm runway of 16,384 taxels at 2-mm pitch.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:37.200
Each module is a flexible electrode array beneath a piezoresistive film, read by a dedicated Teensy-based board.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.800
Assembly: sensor connection, array alignment, and application of the piezoresistive film and waterproof cloth.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.600
Rather than scanning every taxel, each board measures 200 of its 4,096 taxels per frame.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:53.800
Active contacts are tracked, a coarse-to-fine scan monitors the module, and loaded regions are resolved at full density.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.600
Recorded acquisition frames: paw contacts are resolved completely, while unloaded regions are sampled sparsely.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.800
Adaptive sampling enables acquisition across 16,384 taxels at approximately 1,000 frames per second.

00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:11.600
With synchronized video, contacts are assigned to individual paws, correctly identifying 93.3% of 2,469 manually annotated contacts.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:18.700
The load on each paw is then resolved at approximately 1 kHz throughout every stance.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:23.400
After sciatic nerve pinch, rats continued to place the injured hindpaw on the ground...

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.800
...but the loading ratio decreased by approximately 60%, nearly twice the decrease in the duty-factor ratio.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:30.600
In three injured and two sham-operated rats followed for two weeks, both ratios decreased and then returned toward baseline.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.800
Peak loading of the injured hindpaw decreased by 27%, and that of the contralateral hindpaw increased by 22–23%.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:40.700
Contact morphology also changed: injured-hindpaw contacts became smaller and more elongated (cyan outlines).

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.900
The modular design allows the same four modules to form other arenas, such as a square open field with three rats.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:50.700
Future applications include integration with pose estimation, neural recordings, contact-triggered perturbations and shear sensing.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:59.600
Open resources: hardware design files, firmware, analysis software and 85 recordings. tactilearena.github.io

